WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
PAWAN SINHA: There's a
really compelling link

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.160
between neuroscience and art.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.170
Neuroscientists want to
understand how the brain works.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:11.580
What information does
the brain need in order

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.860
to recognize things
in the environment?

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.770
Artists, they have
essentially distilled

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:21.960
their sensory experience
into the keys that enable

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.520
to perceive what to understand.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.320
What it is that they
are trying to depict.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.920
And it's that link that
we hope comes through

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.575
in this class for
all of the students.

00:00:31.575 --> 00:00:33.450
KRISTINE HOCKER: So
vision is really complex.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.075
And this is something
that I've learned

00:00:35.075 --> 00:00:36.600
in previous
neuroscience classes.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.640
But what is really
unique about this class

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.220
is that art allows you
to really slow down

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.590
the process of your
own visual perception.

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:45.690
And so, when you're
doing hands on work

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:48.440
you really get to experience
yourself seeing the world.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.190
SETH RISKIN: Visual
art in the way that we

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:51.990
practice it is quite specific.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:55.230
We create with
materials and light.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:58.350
And we then perceive
our creations

00:00:58.350 --> 00:00:59.710
and we learn from that.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:01.950
So art has the
capacity to go deep

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.750
into perceptual experience.

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.580
And we work with
the students to gain

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.630
consciousness of that process.

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:08.730
SARAH SCHWETTMANN: The
class has two components

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:09.897
that complement one another.

00:01:09.897 --> 00:01:11.040
A studio and a seminar.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.530
In the seminar, we cover
our current understanding

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:14.730
of visual neuroscience.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.890
How the brain makes
sense of incoming data.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.350
Then we translate that
scientific understanding

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:21.420
into hands-on practice
in the studio.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.190
Over the course of
the semester, students

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:26.340
start to develop individual
artworks for exhibition.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:29.730
SETH RISKIN: Each student
decides what area of perception

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:30.480
draws them.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.780
And what they're going to probe
through their own artistic

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.900
experimentation.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:37.650
KRISTINE HOCKER: My
piece shows the viewer

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:41.970
how important visual cues are
for experiencing the world.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:45.300
So what I wanted to do
was suspend an object

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.880
in the Ganzfeld, which is
a space where everything

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.130
is the same color and
the same brightness

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:51.900
and make it disappear.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.110
What was striking to me is
how similar total lightness

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:56.850
and total darkness are.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:00.090
Your brain needs to perceive
things like edges and depth

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.880
and shadows to make sense
of what it's seeing.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.360
Otherwise, it's like you're
looking into total darkness.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.370
SETH RISKIN: Art is
a curious method.

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:10.199
We create things
in the world to see

00:02:10.199 --> 00:02:11.970
what they have to say about us.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.190
This is the way we
use art in the course.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.840
Not as corroboration
of scientific theory.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.770
But as companion
and at times as lead

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:23.360
to discoveries on the
nature of perception.

